 denominator, which is $1-\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{2}{1+\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\text{i})}{(1+\\text{i})(1-\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\text{i})}{{1}^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\text{i})}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\text{i})}{2} \\\\  & =1-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=1-\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=1$ is positive and $y=-1$ is negative, $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-1}{1}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1 \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}))\n$$" 13194139533920 true] [17592186266034 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266035 13194139533920 true] [17592186266034 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266036 13194139533920 true] [17592186266034 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266037 13194139533920 true] [17592186266034 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266038 13194139533920 true] [17592186266034 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266039 13194139533920 true] [17592186266034 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533920 true] [17592186266034 1041 "selection" 13194139533920 true] [17592186266034 1104 #edb/ref 17592186266036 13194139533920 true] [17592186266034 1110 #uuid "a6bbc968-c9c1-56e3-a177-4ae86e3b8b16" 13194139533920 true] [17592186266035 1034 "z=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))" 13194139533920 true] [17592186266035 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533920 true] [17592186266035 1111 #uuid "5be99101-da47-5b60-a7ff-1d4866ac49dd" 13194139533920 true] [17592186266036 1034 "z=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}))" 13194139533920 true] [17592186266036 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533920 true] [17592186266036 1111 #uuid "4931231f-8fe2-5d63-930a-9fad33a60565" 13194139533920 true] [17592186266037 1034 "z=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))" 13194139533920 true] [17592186266037 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533920 true] [17592186266037 1111 #uuid "d395ff26-bd69-5d3e-a7a3-80b698bce481" 13194139533920 true] [17592186266038 1034 "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" 13194139533920 true] [17592186266038 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533920 true] [17592186266038 1111 #uuid "e383b42a-a1f0-518f-85ba-2169a6672380" 13194139533920 true] [17592186266039 1034 "z=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))" 13194139533920 true] [17592186266039 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533920 true] [17592186266039 1111 #uuid "f6eebcb8-c6f5-5f89-b376-0b5ac080a01f" 13194139533920 true] [17592186266040 1017 #edb/ref 17592186266041 13194139533920 true] [17592186266040 1089 "q-86606" 13194139533920 true] [17592186266040 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533920 true] [17592186266040 1100 "Express $z=\\frac{5}{4+3\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give $\\theta $ in radians to three decimal places." 13194139533920 true] [17592186266040 1113 #uuid "9b4fd445-6363-58ec-83c1-a1c688bd3d69" 13194139533920 true] [17592186266040 1145 "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $4-3\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{5}{4+3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(4-3\\text{i})}{(4+3\\text{i})(4-3\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(4-3\\text{i})}{{4}^{2}-9{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(4-3\\text{i})}{16+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(4-3\\text{i})}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}+\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16+9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $ represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{4}{5}$ is positive and $y=-\\frac{3}{5}$ is negative, $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{5})}{(\\frac{4}{5})}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & ≈5.640,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.640)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.640)\n$$" 13194139533920 true] [17592186266041 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266042 13194139533920 true] [17592186266041 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266043 13194139533920 true] [17592186266041 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266044 13194139533920 true] [17592186266041 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266045 13194139533920 true] [17592186266041 1000 #edb/re